    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "St. Stephens served as Alabama Territory's capital from 1817 to 1819 before the government moved to Cahaba.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/old-st-stephens/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1789 Spain built Fort San Esteban near St. Stephens, which later fell under U.S. control.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/old-st-stephens/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Yellow fever epidemics in the 1820s and 1830s, combined with other factors, led St. Stephens to decline.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/old-st-stephens/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Stephens Historical Park preserves the old town site and is listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
